// This file exhaustively demonstrates every construct handled by
// syntax/note.vim, ftplugin/note.vim, and autoload/note.vim. Open it with
// the plugin installed to see each group highlighted.

## Titles

## This whole line is a title
  ## Titles may be indented and still match

## Lists

- dash item
* star item
+ plus item
  - nested dash item
    * nested star item

## Inline emphasis

*bold text*
~strikethrough text~
`inline code`
$highlighted text$
#alert text#
!warning text!
^flagged text^
%percent text%

Emphasis markers also render as bare punctuation when not paired: * ~ ` $ # ! ^ %

## Line breaks

Three consecutive characters on their own act as a horizontal rule:

===
~~~
***

## Fenced code blocks

```
this text is not otherwise processed for note syntax
it can span multiple lines
```

## Comments

/** a block comment, can span multiple lines and contains TODO markers **/

// a line comment, also contains a TODO marker

## Links

http://example.com
https://example.com/path?query=1
file:///Users/example/notes/todo.note

## Arrows

start --> end
end <-- start

## TODO markers

TODO this line contains a bare TODO marker, highlighted anywhere it appears

## Checklists (plain text convention, no dedicated syntax group)

[ ] unchecked
[x] checked
[-] in progress

## Blocks

Blocks are titled regions delimited by a matching bracket pair, a colon,
and an opening brace, closed by a bare `}`. Fold them all with `za`
(bound to note#squish()) and unfold them all with `zoa`
(note#unsquish()).

[a bracket-titled block]: {
  content inside a bracket block
}

<an angle-titled block>: {
  content inside an angle block
}

(a paren-titled block): {
  content inside a paren block
}

[a block title | with a pipe separator](and a paren aside) <and an angle aside>: {
  block titles may mix delimiter types and inline emphasis like *this* or `this`
}

[nested blocks]: {
  [outer]: {
    * list item inside outer
    [inner]: {
      * list item inside inner
    }
  }
}
